{
    "file": "Telephone_Etiquette_for_Customer_Service.docx",
    "title": "Telephone Etiquette for Customer Service",
    "duration": "3 Days",
    "overview": [
        "Telephone interactions remain a critical part of customer service because the customer forms an impression of the organization almost entirely through the employee’s voice, language, listening ability, confidence, accuracy, and responsiveness. Without visual cues, small weaknesses in tone, clarity, questioning, hold procedures, transfers, or call closure can quickly create confusion and dissatisfaction.",
        "This course develops the professional telephone skills required to create positive, efficient, and customer-focused interactions. Participants will learn how to prepare for calls, create a strong first impression, use voice effectively, listen actively, ask purposeful questions, control and structure conversations, provide clear information, manage holds and transfers, take accurate messages, handle complaints and difficult callers, and close calls professionally.",
        "The program emphasizes practical application through telephone scenarios, voice and listening exercises, call-structure practice, difficult-caller simulations, message-taking activities, and call-quality review. Participants will develop a consistent telephone approach that strengthens professionalism, customer confidence, and service quality."
    ],
    "audience": [
        "Customer-service representatives, call-center and contact-center agents, receptionists, front-office employees, administrative assistants, sales-support personnel, help-desk employees, switchboard operators, service coordinators, and any employee who communicates with customers, clients, suppliers, or members of the public by telephone."
    ],
    "methodology": [
        "The course uses an interactive methodology combining short presentations, telephone role plays, voice and tone exercises, active-listening practice, questioning drills, call-structure simulations, hold and transfer scenarios, message-taking exercises, complaint and difficult-caller simulations, call-quality reviews, peer feedback, and personal action planning. Participants repeatedly practice realistic calls so that professional telephone behaviors become practical and consistent."
    ],
    "competencies": [
        "Professional telephone communication",
        "Voice and tone management",
        "Active listening",
        "Effective questioning",
        "Call control",
        "Customer-focused communication",
        "Telephone complaint handling",
        "Difficult-caller management",
        "Accurate message taking",
        "Hold and transfer management",
        "Call documentation",
        "Telephone service quality"
    ],
    "objectivesIntro": [
        "By the end of the course, participants will be able to:"
    ],
    "objectives": [
        "Explain the principles of professional telephone etiquette and their effect on customer perception.",
        "Create a confident and professional first impression when answering and making calls.",
        "Use tone, pace, volume, articulation, and language effectively.",
        "Apply active-listening and questioning techniques to understand customer needs accurately.",
        "Structure and control telephone conversations without making customers feel rushed.",
        "Provide clear information and manage customer expectations professionally.",
        "Use appropriate procedures for holds, transfers, callbacks, and message taking.",
        "Handle complaints, emotional callers, and difficult telephone situations calmly.",
        "Close calls with clear confirmation of actions and next steps.",
        "Apply practical call-quality standards to improve consistency and customer satisfaction."
    ],
